So, we have reached our destination

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Hi, do you speak English -Yes

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Continue this road. It is not much too see I'm sorry

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:45.000
I think it is after number 65 perhaps? - Thank you very much

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.000
So, somewhere near number 65

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.000
And then you basically take "Gruvagen"

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.000
no sorry, it is called "Faltspatsvargen"

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Thank you very much

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.000
We're getting there now

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.000
Getting close now, and all the locals know of it

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.000
So it must be big. It must be famous

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Pull over there

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Ah, Yttery "Gruva"

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:25.000
So it's the birthplace of four elements on the periodic table.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:31.000
Yttrium, Ytterbium, Erbium and Terbium.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:37.000
Ytterby mine, a historic chemical landmark, in my best Swedish

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:44.000
So here we see Yttrium, Ytterbium, Terbium, Erbium

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.000
All found for the first time form minerals, from Gadolinite, in this quarry

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.000
And we were told by a nice gentleman down the road that we can follow the path up through the woods

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.000
and hopefully see the mine... so let's see if we can go find it

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.000
Don't fall over

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.000
So we've reached the top

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.000
Where are we? -Look up yonder. To the left

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.000
Oh wow

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.000
We got to scramble up there then?

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:39.000
So this is excellent as a chemist. For me to come here and see where these elements first came from

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.000
is really quite cool

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.000
Here we can see evidence of the mining. Now this mine closed may years ago

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:51.000
but you can see where the miners have drilled using hand drills into these rocks

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:56.000
to make holes to put explosives in to break away the minerals.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:05.000
Very early on, late 1700s, a naval officer called Arrhenius visited the quarry

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:13.000
I think he ay have been prospecting for fuel, and he found a black rock he thought may have been coal.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:18.000
When he picked the rock up he found it was far too heavy to be coal. 

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:24.000
Coal is simply carbonaceous mineral but this rock was obviously too heavy to be coal

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.000
and he thought there must be something strange about it

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:32.000
He wondered if it might be a new ore of tungsten which had recently been found

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:40.000
So he passed this material to a colleague or a professor at the nearby Abo University for analysis

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:44.000
So that's where the chemistry of the rare earths as they're called, or the lanthanides, started

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:49.000
with the discovery of four new elements from that original black mineral rock.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:54.000
We have found the quarry and it's breathtaking

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:59.000
I'm surprised that we're able to come because everyone told us it was fenced off and closed

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.000
We didn't see any fences

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:12.000
Anyway, somewhere here is a sign which is commemorative of this site so let's see if we can find this historic landmark site.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:20.000
So we have now come up the side of the quarry into the sunlight

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:27.000
to find the original plaque from the American Society of Minerals is in situ

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:35.000
So here it says the ASM has designated Ytterby mine a historical landmark

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:41.000
For the discovery of four periodic elements - Yttrium, Terbium, Erbium and Ytterbium

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:47.000
And that's actually in chronological order. Yttrium was the first one isolated from the black rock

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:53.000
And over a period of the next 40 to 50 years successive scientists re-analyzed that sample

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:56.000
And were able to extract both Terbium and Erbium initially 

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:03.000
Leading to the final isolation of Ytterbium in its purest from way back in 1950

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:11.000
A period of 100 years of chemistry summed up in this sign from this one specific quarry in Sweden

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:13.000
Captions by Brady Haran

